## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to combine streaming sketches with efficient top-K data structures while reasoning about accuracy, space, and time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Processing unbounded data with limited memory
- **Count-Min Sketch (CMS):** Probabilistic data structure for frequency estimation with false positives but no false negatives
- **Top-K Maintenance:** Using a min-heap to track the K most frequent elements efficiently
- **Amortized Analysis:** Bounding average cost over sequences of operations
- **Space-Time Trade-offs:** Comparing approximate vs. exact approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
class CountMinSketch {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w;
    this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => Array(w).fill(0));
  }

  hash(key, i) {
    const str = typeof key === 'string' ? key : String(key);
    let hash = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < str.length; j++) {
      hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(j)) >>> 0;
    }
    return (hash + i * 31) % this.w;
  }

  update(key) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      this.table[i][j]++;
    }
  }

  estimate(key) {
    let min = Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][j]);
    }
    return min;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis for dynamic frequency tracking, demonstrates how to monitor hot keys efficiently, and contrasts incremental updates with full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized analysis for batched updates
- Frequency buckets for O(1) max-frequency queries
- Hash maps for per-label counts
- Sparse vs. dense label domains
- Space-time tradeoff analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
class HotKeyTracker {
  constructor(initialCounts) {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.maxFreq = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(initialCounts)) {
      this.cnt.set(k, v);
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, v);
    }
  }

  update(label, delta) {
    const old = this.cnt.get(label) || 0;
    const freqCount = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
    if (freqCount === 1) this.freq.delete(old);
    else this.freq.set(old, freqCount - 1);

    const nw = old + delta;
    this.cnt.set(label, nw);
    this.freq.set(nw, (this.freq.get(nw) || 0) + 1);
    this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, nw);
  }

  hasHotKey(threshold) {
    return this.maxFreq >= threshold;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic triangle counting with tight amortized analysis, batching, and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic graphs, incremental triangles
- Degree-based edge orientation, forward neighbor sets
- Amortized analysis, batch processing, space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
// high-level sketch
const Nplus = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> new Set());
const deg = new Int32Array(n);
let triangles = 0;
function addEdge(u,v){
  // decide orientation
  if (deg[u] > deg[v] || (deg[u]===deg[v] && u>v)) [u,v] = [v,u];
  // count new triangles
  let t = 0;
  for (let w of Nplus[u]) if (Nplus[v].has(w)) t++;
  triangles += t;
  Nplus[u].add(v);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
  // potentially rebalance orientation lazily
}
```

